{"conditionalSetIds":[],"forecasterId":"demographics-ai-2036:run1:rstar:audit","informationSetArtifactId":"sha256:9bd9a95b9482402f8323632891c46f4e4ae9f1635db6f8fa427ea3bac87eda4b","modelForecastIds":[],"modelUse":"none","prediction":{"kind":"binary","positiveOutcomeId":"yes","probability":0.23},"questionHash":"sha256:cfe468fa9439bedeee3804a35583667745f13831322e2c91a89c02a1b211413b","questionId":"demographics-ai-2036-cq5","questionVersion":"1.0.0","rationale":"CONJUNCT A (ICE BofA MOVE avg >=105, 2027-2036): marginal 48%, driven mostly by INDEX CONSTRUCTION. MOVE is the curve-weighted average of normalized implied vol on 1-month Treasury options (20/20/40/20 across 2y/5y/10y/30y) expressed in BASIS POINTS of annualized yield vol. It is therefore LEVEL-DEPENDENT: bp vol scales roughly with the yield level, so MOVE at 105 with 4.5-5% nominals is the same RELATIVE uncertainty as MOVE at ~60-70 with 2% nominals. If the fiscal-dominance path in my CQ4 holds, MOVE clears 105 with no increase in genuine rate uncertainty at all. That is worth ~10-15pp of probability, upward, and a naive forecaster comparing to the 2010s average (~70-80) will badly under-forecast. Second construction point: a 10-year ARITHMETIC mean of a right-skewed series is pulled up by spikes — one 2023-style year averaging 130 lifts the decade mean ~4-5 points. Third: index continuity. The MOVE has had methodology/ownership changes (Merrill -> ICE, tenor weighting and CMT normalization revisions); a 2027-2036 average spliced across a methodology change is not a single series. Long-run context: ~100 in the 1990s, ~95 in the 2000s, ~70-80 in the 2010s, 200 spike in 2023, ~70-85 in 2025-26. 48%.\nCONJUNCT B (a >=25% MSCI World peak-to-trough drawdown in which G7 GDP-weighted 10y NOMINALS fail to fall >=50bp): marginal 33%. Decomposed: P(any >=25% MSCI World drawdown in 10 years) = 78%. Base rate: 1973-74, 1987, 2000-02, 2007-09, 2020, 2022 (~26% in USD) = ~6 in 53 years, i.e. ~65-70% over a decade; lifted t